      "name": "DispatchSignalHandler",
      "signature": "int DispatchSignalHandler(int nSignal)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "int",
      "comment": "Dispatches registered signal handlers for POSIX signals.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Map signal number to handler pointer location (global or TLS-based)\n2. For SIGINT(2), SIGTERM(15), SIGBREAK(21), SIGABRT(22): use global handlers\n3. For SIGILL(4), SIGFPE(8), SIGSEGV(11): use TLS handler table\n4. If handler is SIG_IGN (1), return 0 (success)\n5. If handler is SIG_DFL (0), call __exit(3)\n6. Save/restore signal state around handler invocation\n7. Clear handler entry after dispatch (one-shot semantics)\n8. Invoke handler with signal number (or signal+code for SIGFPE)\n\nParameters:\n- nSignal (int): Signal number to dispatch\n\nReturns:\n- 0 on success, -1 if signal not recognized\n\nMagic Numbers:\n- 0x2: SIGINT (Ctrl+C interrupt)\n- 0x4: SIGILL (illegal instruction)\n- 0x8: SIGFPE (floating-point exception)\n- 0xB (11): SIGSEGV (segmentation fault)\n- 0xF (15): SIGTERM (termination request)\n- 0x15 (21): SIGBREAK (Ctrl+Break)\n- 0x16 (22): SIGABRT (abort signal)\n- 0x8C (140): FPE_INTDIV (integer divide by zero)\n- 0xC (12): Handler entry stride size\n\nRelated Functions:\n- FindHandlerEntryById: Locates TLS handler entry\n- LockCriticalSectionByIndex: Thread synchronization\n- HandleAbnormalTermination: Caller for SIGABRT",
